{"video_id": "77km5ikcqA8", "source_file": "The Fastest Rate Hike Cycle in Recent History Is Finally Revealing Its Consequences [77km5ikcqA8].mp4", "source_sha256": "cee5f171dafa09ff86e5d80d7136d12eb5e2c87a1d100e49684edb3e04ee04c0", "local_dur": 70.77, "model": "base", "engine": "openai-whisper", "status": "ASR_UNCERTIFIED", "device": "cpu", "elapsed_s": 6.4, "language": "en", "text": " In 2021, a software company called Pluralsight was acquired by private equity firm Vista Equity Partners and Vista did what private equity firms do. They funded the acquisition with a few billion dollars of equity and then over a billion dollars in private credit loans for a total price tag of over three billion dollars. And when a deal like this happens, the acquired company takes on the debt. So Pluralsight's balance sheet went from its own independent structure to suddenly carrying over a billion dollars of new debt from private credit. And this debt just happened to be in the form of floating rate loans pegged to Sofer, which was fine for about three years, because while Sofer was still near zero, the math worked. But then Sofer went from zero to over 5 percent. And suddenly, Pluralsight was underwater. And by August 2024, it was over for Pluralsight. Their interest payments were now unsustainable. And a group of private credit lenders were forced to step in, convert their debt to equity, and inject an additional $275 million of new money into the business. And as their reward for doing so, they took 85 percent of the company. And this is just a single example of the rising rate problem that's quietly spreading across the private credit market.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 6.44, "text": " In 2021, a software company called Pluralsight was acquired by private equity firm Vista Equity", "tokens": [50364, 682, 7201, 11, 257, 4722, 2237, 1219, 2149, 374, 1124, 397, 390, 17554, 538, 4551, 10769, 6174, 691, 5236, 47675, 50688], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logpr